# Tomasulo Algorithm Simulator (Microprocessors)

Developed a GUI-based simulator for the Tomasulo dynamic scheduling algorithm as part of a Microprocessors course.
The simulator executes MIPS-like assembly instructions cycle by cycle, visualizing instruction issue, execution,
and write-back while tracking reservation stations, buffers, register files, cache state, and the common data bus.

Implemented support for floating-point and integer operations, load/store instructions, and branches without
branch prediction. The simulator correctly handles RAW, WAR, and WAW data hazards, address conflicts, and
simultaneous write-back scenarios. Cache behavior is modeled with configurable cache size, block size, hit latency,
and miss penalty, considering compulsory and conflict misses for data memory.

Users can configure instruction latencies, reservation station sizes, buffer capacities, and initial register
values before simulation. The project emphasizes accurate modeling of instruction-level parallelism, pipeline
behavior, and memory hierarchy interactions using a clear, table-based GUI representation.

